.TH CHMAP_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_declare_scoped \- declare a hash map handle that is destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_declare_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name. It also declares the companion
type variables. These variables carry key_t and val_t to every later
type-inferred macro. The macro also attaches the cleanup attribute. This
attribute destroys the map at every exit from the enclosing scope. The
macro creates nothing. The handle holds NULL until one of the init macros
runs.

It is different from chmap_declare(3) in exactly three ways. It sets the
handle to NULL, and does not leave it uninitialized. It sets both
companion type variables to NULL as well. It gives the handle a cleanup
attribute, so an exit from the scope destroys the map that the handle
names at that moment. The handle starts as NULL, and the library ignores
a NULL handle. A return before an init macro runs is therefore safe, and
nothing in the block reads an indeterminate object.

Use this macro with chmap_init(3) or one of its variants when the code
decides the configuration of the map inside the scope.
chmap_construct_scoped(3) is the one-step form for a map whose
configuration is known at the declaration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key and, with
         val_t, which implementation strategy the init macro selects
val_t    Value type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
An explicit chmap_destroy(3) before the end of the scope stays correct.
It sets the handle to NULL, and the cleanup at scope exit then does
nothing
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return the handle. Do not
store it somewhere that outlives the scope. Both of these hand out a
pointer that is freed at the exit from the scope
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle. A function
that gets the handle therefore needs chmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_declare_scoped(scores, char *, int);
if (!load_config()) {
    return -1;             /* nothing was created; cleanup is a no-op */
}
chmap_init(scores);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
return 0;                  /* scores is destroyed here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_construct_scoped (3),
.BR chmap_init (3),
.BR chmap_init_full (3),
.BR chmap_redeclare (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
